"dolphin_controls.mst — Dolphin's OWN comctl32 controls (DolphinDart DD11).

 A shell owning a real `SysTreeView32` and a real `SysListView32`, both
 created by Dolphin's `View>>create` and subclassed by its own
 `ControlView>>subclassWindow` — the same substrate `UI.TextEdit` uses, which
 is the point: an EDIT control and a ListView differ only in which class
 comctl registered, not in how Dolphin gets into their message stream.

 THE PREREQUISITE IS IN THE DOOR, not here. `InitCommonControlsEx` runs on
 the class-registration path (`EnsureTopClass`), because the first control
 CREATED decides whether the whole wave works and a lazily-hit call can land
 on the wrong thread. Without it CreateWindowExW for SysListView32 fails with
 ERROR_CANNOT_FIND_WND_CLASS — a plain creation failure with nothing to say
 that a registration step was missed.

 The gate asks WINDOWS for each control's class name rather than trusting the
 Smalltalk class: a control that quietly fell back to the door's own window
 class would satisfy every other assertion."
ShellView subclass: ControlsShell [
    | tree list |

    tree [ ^tree ]
    list [ ^list ]

    ControlsShell class >> routeDolphinMessages [ ^UiSession routeMessagesFrom: View ]

    build [
        tree := self makeControl: TreeView.
        list := self makeControl: ListView.
        self layoutManager: (BorderLayout new
            west: tree;
            center: list;
            yourself).
        ^self
    ]

    "Same three sends as every other sub-view in this port. `addSubView:` is
     what realizes it — and for a common control that is also where the
     subclassing happens, through `ControlView>>basicCreateWindow:`."
    makeControl: aViewClass [
        | v |
        v := aViewClass new.
        v parentView: self.
        v preferredExtent: 180 @ 200.
        v usePreferredExtent: true.
        self addSubView: v.
        ^v
    ]

    "Windows' answer, not ours."
    classNameOf: aView [ ^User32 getClassName: aView handle ]

    "Did Dolphin's own subclassing run on a COMMON control? `oldWndProc` is
     comctl's procedure, saved by `ControlView>>subclassWindow`; the procedure
     now installed should be the door's trampoline."
    isSubclassed: aView [
        ^aView oldWndProc notNil
            and: [ (User32 getWindowLongPtr: aView handle nIndex: -4) = VM getWndProc ]
    ]
]
